  # ─── Foundational Planat-and-collaborators series ────────────────────────
  # ARS rests on the analytical scaffolding developed in this series: Farey-rational
  # phase locking, Ramanujan-Fourier analysis of arithmetical signals, and the
  # connection of arithmetic to oscillator phase noise. Records verified 2026-09-30.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Planat
        given-names: Michel
      - family-names: Henry
        given-names: Edouard
    title: "The arithmetic of 1/f noise in a phase locked loop"
    collection-title: "Proceedings of the 2002 IEEE International Frequency Control Symposium"
    start: 739
    end: 743
    year: 2002
    doi: "10.1109/FREQ.2002.1075978"
    notes: "Farey / continued-fraction organization of mode locking and 1/f noise in a PLL; ARS reuses this organization as the PLL-bank parameterization."
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Planat
        given-names: Michel
      - family-names: Rosu
        given-names: Haret C.
      - family-names: Perrine
        given-names: Serge
    title: "Ramanujan sums for signal processing of low-frequency noise"
    journal: "Physical Review E"
    volume: 66
    start: "056128"
    year: 2002
    doi: "10.1103/PhysRevE.66.056128"
    notes: "Ramanujan-Fourier transform of arithmetical signals; ARS Engine 1 (ramanujan_fourier) implements this directly."
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Planat
        given-names: Michel
    title: "Huyghens, Bohr, Riemann and Galois: Phase-locking"
    journal: "International Journal of Modern Physics B"
    volume: 20
    start: 1833
    end: 1850
    year: 2006
    url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0510044"
    notes: "Phase locking across all harmonic resonances, 1/f noise, the Mangoldt function and the Riemann zeta function; the analytical anchor for ARS's Riemann-side validation."
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Planat
        given-names: Michel
    title: "Painlevé Confluence and 1/f Phase-Locking Dynamics: A Topological Framework for Human–AI Collaboration"
    journal: "Machine Learning and Knowledge Extraction"
    volume: 8
    issue: 3
    start: 73
    year: 2026
    doi: "10.3390/make8030073"
    notes: "Bost–Connes phase transition as a framework for the lock-in phase hypothesis; the hypothesis ARS's Phase 12 perturbation sweep was originally framed against (Phase 13 reinterprets the Phase 12 signal as a peak-detection artifact, not a test of it)."
